Parameterise the donor in theta, for G4 (M6a/M6b).
=================================================

Apply ONLY in the disposable clone, ONLY for the mutation test:

    cd /home/user/donor-smoke
    git apply /path/to/theta-mutation.patch

Never in the reference clone, and never before M5b. The golden replay joins
on `effect_label`, so it must run against the unpatched donor.

This is a diff, not vendored source (D5: the donor has no licence). It is
recorded here because the mutation it enables is part of the method, and a
mutation nobody can reproduce is not evidence.

WHY IT IS A STRICT SUPERSET
---------------------------
Run with no new flags, the patched generator produces byte-identical output
to the unpatched one: theta 0.0 still labels "null", theta 0.075 still
labels "effect". Only a theta outside that pair gets a new label. So the
patch cannot invalidate the golden replay even if applied by accident -- it
can only add.

WHAT EACH HUNK IS FOR, AGAINST THE SIX G4 CRITERIA
--------------------------------------------------
1. generate_panels.R, --effect_sizes flag          -> criterion 1
2. generate_panels.R, magnitude-bearing labels     -> the structural defect:
     effect_labels are directory names, so a second theta silently
     overwrites the first. Blocking for the atlas at M7.
3. plot_forest.py    -> criterion 5 (defect D7)
4. plot_ci_gallery.py -> criterion 5 (defect D7)

Hunks 3 and 4 are the ones that matter most. Without them a theta = +2% run
computes correct numbers and draws figures whose reference line is still at
7.5%, making every estimator look massively biased when the error is in the
axis. They read the truth from `metadata.json`, which the generator already
writes and which already carries `effect_sizes`.

Criteria 2, 4 and 6 need no patch -- see docs/donor-repro.md 5a for why.

--- a/src/R/generate_panels.R
+++ b/src/R/generate_panels.R
@@ -38,8 +38,7 @@
 # Treatment effect
 # Set to 0.0 for null condition (false positive rate test)
 # Set to 0.075 for 7.5% lift (power/accuracy test)
-effect_sizes <- c(0.0, 0.075)
-effect_labels <- c("null", "effect")
+effect_sizes <- c(0.0, 0.075)

 # Panel dimensions
 total_days <- 105 # Total simulation length in days
@@ -113,6 +112,28 @@
 n_iterations <- as.integer(parse_cli("--n_iterations", n_iterations))
 output_base  <- parse_cli("--output_base", "panels")

+# --effect_sizes 0,0.02  (comma-separated proportions; negatives allowed)
+effect_sizes <- as.numeric(strsplit(
+  parse_cli("--effect_sizes", paste(effect_sizes, collapse = ",")), ","
+)[[1]])
+if (any(is.na(effect_sizes))) {
+  stop("--effect_sizes must be comma-separated numbers, e.g. 0,0.02 or 0,-0.05")
+}
+
+# Labels are directory names and the arm key throughout, so they have to
+# carry the magnitude: otherwise a second theta overwrites the first and two
+# effect sizes cannot coexist on disk.
+#
+# The donor's two values keep their published names exactly, so a default
+# run is byte-identical and the golden replay is unaffected.
+# Resolution is one part in a thousand (eff_p020 == +2.0%), which is finer
+# than any theta this study will use; two values that collide are caught
+# below rather than silently sharing a directory.
+theta_label <- function(x) {
+  if (isTRUE(all.equal(x, 0.0)))   return("null")
+  if (isTRUE(all.equal(x, 0.075))) return("effect")
+  sprintf("eff_%s%03d", if (x < 0) "m" else "p", round(abs(x) * 1000))
+}
+effect_labels <- vapply(effect_sizes, theta_label, character(1))
+if (anyDuplicated(effect_labels)) {
+  stop("--effect_sizes produced duplicate labels: ",
+       paste(effect_labels, collapse = ", "))
+}
+
